From c13ab62d1f8f1fa5cc8a171d3a246fc531427b6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:47:07 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Channel Planning] Channel Preference Report handling with
 Driver Rank

Three device_conf knobs control the channel-preference-report behaviour.
Two of them were already declared in AgentDB and consumed by
ChannelSelectionTask but were never plumbed through config parsing or
validated in agent main; a third knob is added here to let the customer
choose whether the unsolicited report is change-gated or emitted on every
cache update. This change completes the end-to-end wiring so all three
knobs are configurable and enforced for every combination.

Knobs (all in the [global] section of beerocks_agent.conf):
  - chansel_preference_report_refresh_enable
      Rebuild + normalize the local channel-preference table (rank 1..15,
      per-radio max-rank anchor) from the driver ranking on every
      CBS / ACS / DYNAMIC-ACS completion, independent of the
      channel-selection approach knobs (enable_auto_chansel_handling /
      cbs_based_chansel_enable / active_acs_chansel_enable). Keeps a fresh
      normalized preference table available for proprietary channel
      selection and for the values reported in the Channel Preference
      Report (CPR).
  - chansel_unsolicited_preference_report_enable
      Emit an unsolicited CPR on a ranking cache refresh. Latched to the
      refresh knob: without a fresh normalized table there is nothing
      meaningful to report.
  - unsolicited_preference_report_only_on_cache_update  (default 1)
      Controls WHEN the unsolicited CPR is emitted (only relevant when the
      two knobs above are enabled):
        1 - send only when the normalized preference set CHANGED since the
            last report (change-detected; duplicate refreshes suppressed).
        0 - send on EVERY cache refresh (every CBS / dynamic ACS / ACS
            completion), regardless of whether the preference changed.

Changes:
  - agent_db.h              : add
    unsolicited_preference_report_only_on_cache_update to device_conf
    (default true).
  - beerocks_config_file.h  : add the three std::string members to
    sConfigSlave.
  - beerocks_config_file.cpp: add the three parse tuples to
    slave_global_conf_args so the keys are read from [global].
  - beerocks_agent_main.cpp : convert the config strings into the
    device_conf booleans; validate that unsolicited=1 requires refresh=1
    (otherwise treat unsolicited as disabled and log a WARNING); resolve
    the new only-on-cache-update knob (absent/empty -> default 1); add a
    [CBS] Config debug line dumping resolved + raw values.
  - beerocks_agent.conf.in  : document all three keys; refresh and
    unsolicited default to 0, only_on_cache_update defaults to 1.
  - channel_selection_task.cpp: in handle_vs_channels_list_response(),
    gate the unsolicited-CPR emission on
    unsolicited_preference_report_only_on_cache_update. When 1, keep the
    existing change-detected behaviour (compare against the last reported
    snapshot); when 0, send on every refresh. The last-reported snapshot
    is still updated on send in both modes, so toggling back to 1 resumes
    correct change detection.

MLD link handling:
On an MLD single-PHY setup the driver ACS report and CBS trigger are
per-affiliated-link (hostapd_cli -i <mld_iface> -l <link> ...). The nl80211
HAL was issuing "ACS show_report" and "CBS enable 1" on the base radio
socket WITHOUT a link selector, so every radio (link) read/triggered link 0.
This caused a 5GHz radio to ingest another link's (2.4/6GHz) ranks, and the
per-radio preference table to be built from the wrong band's data and to
differ between two back-to-back refreshes.

Fixes (nl80211 backend only; QMESH/QSDK path):
  - read_acs_ranks(): derive the link id from the radio's link iface via
    extractLinkId() and append "-l <link_id>" to "ACS show_report" on MLD
    interfaces (non-MLD -> link_id -1 -> unchanged plain command).
  - trigger_cbs(): likewise send "CBS -l <link_id> enable 1" on MLD so the
    scan runs on the intended link/band.
  - refresh_preference_table() (backhaul, HAL-agnostic safety net): discard
    any rank entry whose channel is not in this radio's channels_list before
    building the table, so a 5GHz radio can never ingest 2.4/6GHz ranks even
    if a foreign entry leaks in. The normalization anchor and ranked-by-bw
    index use the filtered set. Foreign drops are logged.

All changes are generic: link selection is derived from the iface name (no
hard-coded link indices), the band filter uses the radio's own channel list
(no hard-coded bands), and non-MLD single-link setups are unaffected.

Behaviour matrix (now fully enforced):
  - refresh=0                          : legacy; no normalization / no CPR.
  - refresh=1, unsolicited=0           : table refreshed + normalized on
                                         CBS/ACS/DYNAMIC-ACS; no unsolicited
                                         CPR.
  - refresh=1 & unsolicited=1 &
    only_on_cache_update=1 (default)   : refresh + normalize + unsolicited
                                         CPR emitted only on change.
  - refresh=1 & unsolicited=1 &
    only_on_cache_update=0             : refresh + normalize + unsolicited
                                         CPR emitted on every cache update.
  - unsolicited=1 & refresh=0          : treated as disabled (warned in
                                         agent main).

Note: these knobs are consumed only by the backhaul-side
ChannelSelectionTask (agent/son_slave process, which reads AgentDB
directly), so unlike cbs_based_chansel_enable they are not propagated to
the fronthaul ap_manager process via cACTION_APMANAGER_CONFIGURE. No
TLVF / ap_manager changes are required.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
